rows.reply:
We cannot say whether your application will be approved. Only the responsible authority decides an individual case, and that is the parental allowance office that handles your application. What the law says in general is below.

Under federal law, a person is entitled to Elterngeld (parental allowance) if they meet all four of these conditions:
1. they have a residence or habitual abode in Germany;
2. they live in one household with their child;
3. they care for and raise the child themselves;
4. they work not at all or not full-time.

The law also covers some people who do not meet the residence condition, for example people subject to German social insurance law who are temporarily posted abroad. For twins or other multiple births there is only one entitlement.

Elterngeld must be applied for in writing. Payment is made retroactively only for the three months of the child's life before the month in which the application is received. Earlier months are not paid. The application must state which months you are claiming as Basiselterngeld, Elterngeld Plus or Partnerschaftsbonus. In most cases the applicant signs it, and the other entitled parent also signs to confirm they have seen it.

The official guidance lists the documents usually attached. These are the child's birth certificate and proof of your previous income. For employees, that means pay slips for the 12 months before the relevant point. For self-employed people, it is usually the last tax assessment. More documents may be needed depending on the case.

If you disagree with the decision you receive, the guidance says you can lodge an objection in writing with the same office within one month of receiving it.
